  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Learning Objectives</title>
      <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to…</para>
        <list id="list-00001">
          <item>Compare and contrast the major cognitive-developmental theories</item>
          <item>Identify key changes in the brain across middle adulthood</item>
        </list>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <para id="para-00002">As young toddlers, we begin to see ourselves as a collection of facts, aptitudes, and appearances. These can be basic facts like our name, our family members, or our age. One facet of our identity is our gender. Based on social roles and not on biology, gender allows young toddlers to distinguish between groups of people and to find a meaningful label for themselves.. Recall that you learned about this concept in the discussion on <link document="m00003">Physical Growth</link>.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Identity and Self</title>
      <para id="para-00003">The <term id="term-00001">self</term> is difficult to assess, measure, and investigate, because defining and understanding what we mean by the self is complex. When infants are first born, they have no concept of self; they are unable to distinguish between who they are as compared to who they are not. Their entire world is interconnected, and they are still discovering where they end and their caregiver or their surrounding environment begins (you will learn more about these in [link]Social and Emotional Development in Infants and Toddlers (birth to age 2.5 years)[/link].</para>
      <para id="para-00004">A young infant who is carried, fed, and cuddled by a caregiver first discovers what they have control or <term id="term-00002">autonomy</term> over. Autonomy is the concept of independence or freedom. In the first few weeks of life, an infant may discover that they have complete autonomy over their ability to wiggle their arms and legs, but they do not have control over whether their caregiver picks them up or feeds them. This dichotomy of the <term id="term-00003">self versus other</term> is the beginning of identity development.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">This module tries centering text WW-test without using MathType
        At the age of 12 months, the infant’s self-concept typically does not include their appearance. This is because young infants lack <term id="term-00004">self-recognition</term> which is the ability to recognize and be aware of themselves in reflective surfaces or photos. The <term id="term-00005">Mirror Test</term> was first conducted by Michael Lewis and Jeanne Brooks-Gunn (1979); in this test, a mark of red is made on the infants’ nose without them being aware, and then they are placed in front of a mirror. [link]Figure06_02_Recovery[/link] shows this phenomenona.</para>
        <figure id="fig-00001">
        <media alt="The first map depicting Philadelphia is shown.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpg" src="../../media/Figure06_02_Recovery.jpg"/>
          </media>
        <caption>Created by Thomas Holme in 1863, A Portraiture of the City of Philadelphia was the first map that depicted the Pennsylvania city founded by William Penn and his fellow Quakers. Holme was a surveyor who worked with Penn to devise a system of grid-patterned streets.</caption>
        </figure>
      </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Gender Identity</title>
      <para id="para-00006">Biological sex refers to how we describe physical, anatomical, chromosomal, or genetic variations in our species. Biological sex is often declared and assigned at birth (or before) based on the presence of external genitalia. Infants are typically classified as either male or female, but one in 5,000 births are identified as intersex, which indicates their physical characteristics are neither female nor male. Although most females have two X chromosomes (XX) and most males have one X and one Y chromosome (XY), intersex individuals may have many possible genetic combinations and prenatal hormonal expressions. Timeout is depicted in [link]Figure 06_03_Timeout[/link].</para>
    </section>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
      <media alt="An image shows an engraving of Fort Oswego.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure06_03_Timeout.png"/>
        </media>
      <caption>This 1877 engraving depicts the defeat of the British by combined French and American Indian forces at Fort Oswego in 1756.</caption>
      </figure>
    <para id="para-00007">Gender identity refers to the cognitive, emotional, and social aspects of how we perceive ourselves in comparison to the gender roles of our society. Often, our gender identity is associated with our biological sex, with many males identifying as boys and men, and many females identifying as girls and women. But that is not always the case. Understanding, recognizing, and identifying our own gender is a complex process that takes years to accomplish ([link]Figure 07_01_Concepts[/link]). Gender socialization begins shortly after birth and includes how caregiver select clothing and accessories for young infants.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
      <media alt="An image shows a drawing of Elmina Castle.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure07_01_Concepts.png"/>
        </media>
      <caption>In 1482, Portuguese traders built Elmina Castle (also called São Jorge da Mina, or Saint George’s of the Mine) in present-day Ghana, on the west coast of Africa. Originally the fort was used to trade gold, but by the sixteenth century it was serving as a holding pen for enslaved Africans waiting to board ships that would take them to the Americas. This image of the Elmina dates from the 1660s, when the fort had been seized by Dutch traders.</caption>
      </figure>
      <para id="para-00008">But gender stereotypes go beyond toy selection. Parents of girls tend to perceive them as more delicate and use a soft touch whereas parents of boys tend to perceive them as strong and handle them with a firmer touch (Stern &amp; Hildebrant-Karraker, 1989). Parents also offer differ in the types of vocalizations, conversation style, eye contact, and play behavior that they initiate with infant girls as compared to boys. Finally, parents may enforce gender stereotypes by rewarding infants when they conform to gendered expectations.</para>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
<para id="para-00009">Do you know of a restaurant that was doing really well until it moved into a larger space? Often this happens because the owners thought their profits could handle the costs of the increased space. Unfortunately, they were not really aware of the cost of their food.</para>
</note>
      </section>
